a("ai-incident-database", ent="ai-incident-database", battery=True,
  mt="THE AI INCIDENT DATABASE BY ITS NAME ON THE OVERVIEW — THE OPERATOR AND THE AVIATION MODEL RIGHT; THE MIT TRACKER NAMED AS ITS EXTENSION; A KAGGLE DATASET OF 32 INCIDENTS AMONG THE CARDS",
  d="THE AI INCIDENT DATABASE, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a free, open-source project managed by the Responsible AI Collaborative that indexes real-world harms, near-harms, and failures caused by artificial intelligence systems'. Four points: the aviation and cybersecurity reporting model, a scope of harms (bias, privacy, misinformation, deepfakes, vehicles, safety-critical failures), growth with deepfake and scam entries multiplying, and the MIT AI Incident Tracker as a research extension that parses the database with taxonomies and LLM pipelines. Six cards: the database, the MIT tracker, Partnership on AI, the OECD catalogue entry, a Kaggle dataset, a TIME clip.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview identifies the database, its operator and its purpose, gives the model it is built on and the kinds of harm it holds, and names the MIT tracker that reclassifies it. Its cards are the database's own about page, the tracker, the founding sponsor's page and the OECD's catalogue entry.",
  findings=["THE ENTITY HELD. Operator (the Responsible AI Collaborative), purpose ('harms, near-harms'), the aviation model and the MIT tracker as a derived layer are the record's fields: the about page's operator, the editors' guide's comparison with aviation, and the tracker's own statement that it classifies AIID reports with Arcola AI's pipeline. " + CK,
            "WHAT IS NOT GIVEN. The incident as the unit, the reports attached to it, and the citation page /cite/N are absent; 'collects public and crowdsourced reports' stands in for the unit.",
            "A HOMONYM IN THE CARDS. 'AI Incidents, Failures & Controversies Database' on Kaggle (32 incidents, 29 companies, 13 countries) is another dataset with a near name; the composition does not use it, and the card sits beside the database's own.",
            "'OPEN-SOURCE'. The database's code is open on GitHub (its about page); the record states no data licence on the snapshots page, and the composition's 'free, open-source' does not distinguish the two."],
  analysis="The Overview composes the database at the grain of its own about page and keeps it apart from its neighbours. It gives the operator, the model and the derived layer, and omits the incident-and-report structure that makes the database what it is. This is the baseline for the meta-aggregator's record, which was not yet indexed: no card is machinemediation.org.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="The Responsible AI Collaborative and Partnership on AI named; no person named (the record names Sean McGregor); no identifier or citation page given; the database's own pages cited.")

a("what-counts-as-an-incident-in-the-ai-incident-database", ent="ai-incident-database", battery=True,
  mt="WHAT COUNTS AS AN INCIDENT IN THE AIID, ON THE OVERVIEW — THE DEFINITION VERBATIM; THE EDITORS' FIVE-QUESTION TEST GIVEN AS THREE THRESHOLDS; INCIDENT, ISSUE AND VARIANT KEPT APART",
  d="THE AI INCIDENT DATABASE'S UNIT, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: the definition from the Editor's Guide verbatim ('an alleged harm or near-harm event to people, property, or the environment where an AI system is implicated'), the adaptive criteria, 'three core baseline thresholds' (specific event, AI implication, harm or near-harm), CSET's taxonomy for categories, and the three entry kinds (incident, issue, variant). Nine cards, five of them the database's own pages (the guide, 'Defining an AI Incident', the about page) or papers on it (AAAI, ACM).",
  reading="The innocent reading first. Asked what counts, the Overview answers from the database's own guide: the definition, the threshold for admission, the taxonomy for sorting, and the difference between an incident, an issue that has not occurred and a variant of a known incident.",
  findings=["THE DEFINITION EXACT. The incident, the issue and the variant are the Editor's Guide's definitions (§2.1) in substance, the first verbatim. " + CK,
            "FIVE QUESTIONS MADE THREE. The guide's test asks five things: a specific event, an identifiable AI system, a causal AI role, harm or credible near harm, and adequate evidence (§3). The composition gives three thresholds, folding the identifiable system into 'AI implication' and dropping adequate evidence.",
            "THE REPORT ABSENT AGAIN. As at the database's name, the report as the source attached to an incident is not stated; the unit is the incident alone."],
  analysis="At the question of its unit the Overview reads the database's own guide and keeps the guide's distinctions, with one compression: the five-part admission test arrives as three parts, and the evidence requirement is gone. The record's 'what its selection leaves out' rests on that test; the composition carries the test's harm condition and loses its evidence condition.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="The database and CSET named; no person named; the guide cited and quoted; no citation page given.")

a("papers-with-code", ent="papers-with-code", battery=True,
  mt="PAPERS WITH CODE BY ITS NAME ON THE OVERVIEW — PAST TENSE, THE FOUNDERS AND THE SUNSET RIGHT; THE REDIRECT GIVEN AS HUGGING FACE TRENDING PAPERS; THE REVIVAL AT PAPERSWITHCODE.CO NAMED 'COMMUNITY-DRIVEN'",
  d="PAPERS WITH CODE, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'was a free, open-access platform' linking machine learning papers to code, datasets and benchmarks. History: launched 2018 by Robert Stojnic and Ross Taylor; acquired by Facebook (Meta AI Research) in December 2019; 'Shut down by Meta on July 24, 2025'; the historical dataset on GitHub. Alternatives: 'it initially redirected to Hugging Face Trending Papers'; 'community-driven alternatives like Papers with Code co'. Four cards: the TIB blog, Coursera, a Reddit post on the code opened under Apache 2.0, a video review of PapersWithCode.co.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview tells the history in the past tense, dates the founding, the acquisition and the end, points to the surviving data, and names what users have moved to.",
  findings=["THE STATUS AND THE FOUNDING RIGHT. Founders, 2018, Facebook's ownership from December 2019 and the sunset of 24 July 2025 are the record's fields (TechCrunch; Hugging Face's announcement as hyper.ai reports it). 'Shut down by Meta' is stated outright; the revival's own README says the original was one 'which Meta retired in 2025'. " + CK,
            "THE REDIRECT, TWO WAYS. This composition says the site 'initially redirected to Hugging Face Trending Papers'; the composition at 'is papers with code still active' the same night says it 'redirected users to a static GitHub archive', and the TIB card it cites says users 'were simply redirected to the Papers with Code Github repository'. The record says users were 'pointed to Hugging Face' (CodeSOTA, hyper.ai).",
            "THE REVIVAL NAMED, LIGHTLY. 'Papers with Code co' appears as a community-driven alternative. The revival at paperswithcode.co, by Niels Rogge of Hugging Face's open-source team, its code at github.com/huggingface/paperswithcode under Apache-2.0, was in the record only as an undated individual effort.",
            "THE BATTERY. Ended: yes. Founders and owner apart: yes. Dumps apart from successors: yes. CodeSOTA presented as Papers with Code: no. A current result attributed to the frozen tables: no."],
  analysis="The Overview keeps the dead aggregator dead and its parts apart, passing the record's battery. It also carries a fact the record lacked at the grain it needed: the name has a live instance at a new address. The record is corrected from this capture and the next (see 'is papers with code still active').",
  per_v={"author": True, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": False}, per_note="Robert Stojnic, Ross Taylor, Facebook and Meta named; no identifier; no card is the entity's own page (the original no longer serves).")

a("is-papers-with-code-still-active", ent="papers-with-code", battery=True,
  mt="IS PAPERS WITH CODE STILL ACTIVE, ON THE OVERVIEW — 'YES': THE ORIGINAL SUNSET 24 JULY 2025, THE REVIVAL AT PAPERSWITHCODE.CO BY NIELS ROGGE AT HUGGING FACE, OPEN-SOURCED UNDER APACHE 2.0",
  d="PAPERS WITH CODE'S STATUS, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'Yes, Papers with Code is active, but it has undergone a complete transformation and revival.' The original, acquired by Meta in 2019, was 'abruptly shut down and sunsetted on July 24, 2025', then redirected to a static GitHub archive; 'Hugging Face officially stepped in to revive the platform', a team led by Niels Rogge rebuilding it at paperswithcode.co, open-sourced under Apache 2.0 (FastAPI and React), curated by automated workflows and AI agents, its leaderboards rebuilt from snapshots and still growing. The frozen dump stays on GitHub. Twelve cards: Reddit (four), Niels Rogge on LinkedIn, Hugging Face's blog, the huggingface/paperswithcode repository, CodeSOTA, TIB, Miraheze, QUASA.",
  reading="The innocent reading first. Asked whether it is active, the Overview answers yes and explains why: the original ended, and a rebuilt Papers with Code runs at a new domain from Hugging Face, with its code open, the old data frozen elsewhere.",
  findings=["THE REVIVAL CONFIRMED AT ITS SOURCE. github.com/huggingface/paperswithcode: 'This repository hosts the code of Papers with Code, which tracks trending AI research with code, datasets and leaderboards'; Apache-2.0; its README says it 'recreates the original paperswithcode.com, which Meta retired in 2025' and that the live site is paperswithcode.co. Read 2026-10-29.",
            "'OFFICIALLY'. The composition says Hugging Face 'officially stepped in'. The code sits in Hugging Face's organization and the relaunch is on Hugging Face's blog; AI Weekly described it as an individual effort. The record keeps both.",
            "THE RECORD CORRECTED. The meta-aggregator record of 2026-10-28 gave the revival as undated and individual and the entity as ended without qualification. The original is ended; its name now has a live instance, and the record is corrected on this capture: the revival gets its own entry, and the original's negative tag 'NOT live since 24 July 2025' is scoped to paperswithcode.com. " + CK,
            "THE BATTERY'S FIRST CHECK, RE-READ. 'Status given as ended (sunset 24 July 2025)': the composition gives the sunset and the revival together, which is the state as it stands."],
  analysis="The question the battery asked is answered better than the record answered it. The capture is the composition layer correcting the entity's own formalization: the record had the original's death right and the successor's life at too coarse a grain. This is the first measured case of the meta-aggregator's records taking a correction from a capture.",
  per_v={"author": True, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="Niels Rogge, Hugging Face and Meta named; no identifier; the revival's own repository and blog cited.")

a("oecd-ai-incidents-and-hazards-monitor", ent="oecd-ai-incidents-monitor",
  mt="THE OECD AI INCIDENTS AND HAZARDS MONITOR ON THE OVERVIEW — EVENT REGISTRY, 150,000 ARTICLES A DAY, THE DEFINITIONS RIGHT; THE LLM CLASSIFIERS UNSAID",
  d="THE OECD AIM, BY NAME, ON GOOGLE'S AI OVERVIEW, SIGNED OUT, 2026-10-29: 'a real-time tracking tool developed by the OECD.AI Policy Observatory in collaboration with the Patrick J. McGovern Foundation'; it aggregates world news through Event Registry, 'processing over 150,000 news articles daily'; 14 risk themes; the definitions of incident and hazard. Six cards, five the OECD's own (the monitor, the methodology page, the May 2024 definitions report).",
  reading="The innocent reading first. The Overview names the monitor's maker and supporter, says how it reads the news, sorts what it finds into themes, and defines its two kinds of event.",
  findings=["THE PIPELINE'S INPUT RIGHT, ITS MACHINE UNSAID. Event Registry and the 150,000 articles a day are the methodology page's. That large language models have classified the events since November 2024 and written their titles and summaries (GPT-4o mini, GPT-4o, o3-mini) is not said. The record reads the monitor as itself a machine reception of the news; the composition reads it as an aggregator of the news. " + CK,
            "THE MAKER. The methodology page says the monitor 'was initiated and is being developed by the OECD.AI expert group on AI incidents' with support from the Patrick J. McGovern Foundation; 'developed by the OECD.AI Policy Observatory in collaboration with' is close.",
            "FOURTEEN THEMES. The count comes from a card ('Trends in AI incidents and hazards reported by the media'); the methodology page read gives no count of themes. Not verified here."],
  analysis="The monitor is composed from its own methodology page with its inputs and definitions right and its classifier left out, the part of its method that makes each record a machine composition. The record holds what the composition drops.",
  per_v={"author": False, "inst": True, "id": False, "src": True}, per_note="OECD.AI and the Patrick J. McGovern Foundation named; no person; the OECD's own pages cited.")

